.PP
The reply a handler builds: a status, headers and a body, sent in one piece when the handler returns. Fields are read-only (a test reads code, body and the headers through w.header... of an http.recorder()).
.SS Mux
.EX
struct Mux {
    private MuxRoute *routes;
    private i64 len;
    private i64 cap;
    private u64 subtrees;
    private MuxMw *mws;
    private i64 nmws;
    private i64 mwcap;
    private Mux *parent;
    private u8 *prefix;
    private i64 prefixLen;
    private Mux **groups;
    private i64 ngroups;
    private i64 gcap;
};
.EE
.PP
Routes requests to handlers by method and path, as Go 1.22's ServeMux does. Declare one (\fBhttp.Mux mux;\fR: no init call), add routes with mux.handle, give it to http.listen. Patterns are in route.ktm; the routes are kept so that a more specific pattern comes before a more general one, and since two patterns that could match the same request and are equally specific are refused, the first route that matches is the most specific.
.SS Options
.EX
struct Options {
    i64 threads;
    u8[] bindaddr;
    i64 maxHeaderBytes;
    i64 maxBodyBytes;
    i64 readTimeoutMs;
    i64 idleTimeoutMs;
    bool noSignals;
    bool accessLog;
    Transport *transport;
};
.EE
.PP
How http.listen runs. A declared Options is all zero: every 0 means the default in the comment.
.SS Transport
.EX
struct Transport {
    void *ctx;
    fn(void *ctx, i32 fd, i64 ms) void * start;
    fn(void *conn, u8[] buf, i64 ms) i64 read;
    fn(void *conn, u8[] data, i64 ms) i64 write;
    fn(void *conn) void end;
};
.EE
.PP
A layer over each connection's socket, as TLS: start makes it for an accepted fd (null: drop the connection), read and write move bytes (as coro.read and coro.write: > 0, 0 at the end, -1 on an error or the time limit), end finishes it (the fd is closed after). ctx is start's first argument. net.https makes one over net.tls, which keeps mbedTLS out of programs that don't use it.
