.SS decode
.EX
T decode<T>(Request *r, i64 flags = 0);
.EE
.PP
The body as JSON in a T: \fBUser u = http.decode<User>(r);\fR\&. What it allocated is freed when the handler returns: don't free it, and copy what must live longer. Bad JSON throws json.ERR_DECODE, which the server answers with a 400 and the decode message, so a handler needs no check of its own.
.SS decodeValid
.EX
T decodeValid<T>(Request *r, i64 flags = 0);
.EE
.PP
The body as JSON in a T, checked with validate.check: \fBBookmark b = http.decodeValid<Bookmark>(r);\fR\&. Bad JSON is a 400 as with decode; a value that breaks its fields' rules throws validate.ERR_INVALID, which the server (and a Mux in a test) answers with a 422 and {"error":"invalid request","fields":[{"field":"url","message":"not a URL"}]}.
