.SS Mux.handle
.EX
void Mux.handle(u8[] pattern, Handler h);
.EE
.PP
Adds a route. pattern is "[METHOD ]/path": "GET /users/{id}", "/static/" (a subtree), "POST /files/{path...}", "/{$}" (only "/"). Throws ERR_PATTERN for a pattern that isn't one, or one that conflicts with a route already there.
.SS Mux.use
.EX
void Mux.use(Middleware f);
.EE
.PP
Adds a middleware: every request the Mux serves (404s and 405s too) goes through its middlewares in the order they were added, then the route's handler. On a group: only the group's routes, after the middlewares of the Mux (and the groups) it was made from.
.SS Mux.useWith
.EX
void Mux.useWith(MiddlewareWith f, void *ctx, bool owns = false);
.EE
.PP
Adds a middleware with settings: f(w, r, ctx) for every request, as use(). ctx must outlive the Mux; with owns, the Mux frees it (one mem.alloc'd block) when it is freed.
.SS Mux.group
.EX
Mux *Mux.group(u8[] prefix);
.EE
.PP
A group of routes under prefix ("/api"): its handle("GET /users", h) adds "GET /api/users" to this Mux, and its use() is for its routes only. A group can have groups. It belongs to the Mux: freed by its free(), never by you.
.SS Mux.files
.EX
void Mux.files(u8[] prefix, u8[] dir);
.EE
.PP
Serves the files under dir at prefix ("/static/", which must end in '/'): GET prefix{path...}. See fileServe (files.ktm) for what it does.
.SS Mux.serve
.EX
void Mux.serve(Writer *w, Request *r);
.EE
.PP
Answers r: the most specific route's handler, with the path's wildcards set as r.param(name) (unescaped). Like Go: a path that isn't clean ("//", "/./", "/../") gets a 301 to the clean one, "/dir" a 301 to "/dir/" when only the subtree "/dir/" (or "/dir/{x...}") would match it; no route 404, a route for other methods only 405 + Allow.
.SS Mux.free
.EX
void Mux.free();
.EE
.PP
Frees the routes, middlewares and groups; the Mux is empty again. Nothing on a group: it is freed with the Mux it was made from.
